name: moving data
boss: Plaintext
flavor: Every byte you send bare, I already have.
brief: wget fetches over http. nc opens a raw tcp connection and hands it to
brief: you as stdin and stdout, which is how you speak any text protocol by
brief: hand. The older ftp and tftp clients are here because the machines you
brief: will meet in a bad week are often older than you are.
brief:
brief: This build of nc can connect out but cannot listen -- server mode is
brief: compiled out on purpose. xos opens no doors it was not asked to.
---
q: {download http://@host@/file.txt to a file named after it|download http://@host@/file.txt, saving it under a name taken from the url itself|fetch http://@host@/file.txt and save it under its own basename}
a: wget http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget
hint: wget with no -O saves the file under the url's own basename
---
q: {download http://@host@/file.txt and save it under a name you choose|download http://@host@/file.txt, saving it under a filename you pick|fetch http://@host@/file.txt into a file named however you like}
a: wget -O out.txt http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget -O
hint: -O picks the output filename yourself, instead of using the url's
---
q: {download http://@host@/file.txt quietly, printing nothing|download http://@host@/file.txt with no progress output at all|fetch http://@host@/file.txt silently}
a: wget -q http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget -q
hint: -q silences wget's normal progress output
---
q: {resume an interrupted download of http://@host@/file.txt rather than starting again|pick an interrupted download of http://@host@/file.txt back up instead of restarting it|continue a partial download of http://@host@/file.txt instead of starting over}
a: wget -c http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget -c
hint: -c picks up a partial download where it left off
---
q: {check whether http://@host@/file.txt exists without downloading the body|ask whether http://@host@/file.txt exists without fetching its body|test whether http://@host@/file.txt exists without downloading it}
a: wget --spider http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget --spider
hint: --spider checks the url without saving anything
---
q: {download http://@host@/file.txt showing the server's response headers|download http://@host@/file.txt while printing the server's response headers|fetch http://@host@/file.txt and show the headers the server sent back}
a: wget -S http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget -S
hint: -S prints the server's response headers as they arrive
---
q: {send an extra header with a request for http://@host@/file.txt|add a custom header to a download of http://@host@/file.txt|attach an extra header when fetching http://@host@/file.txt}
a: wget --header 'X-Token: abc' http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget --header
hint: --header adds one arbitrary header line to the request
---
q: {post some data to http://@host@/submit rather than fetching|send a POST request with data to http://@host@/submit instead of downloading|POST data to http://@host@/submit instead of fetching it}
a: wget --post-data 'a=1' http://@host@/submit
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget --post-data
hint: --post-data sends the string as the request body over POST
---
q: {post the contents of @txt@ to http://@host@/submit|POST @txt@'s contents to http://@host@/submit as the request body|send @txt@'s contents in a POST request to http://@host@/submit}
a: wget --post-file @txt@ http://@host@/submit
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget --post-file
hint: --post-file reads the POST body from a file instead of a literal string
---
q: {download http://@host@/file.txt into @dir@|save a download of http://@host@/file.txt into @dir@|fetch http://@host@/file.txt into @dir@}
a: wget -P @dir@ http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget -P
hint: -P sets the directory the download is saved into
---
q: {download http://@host@/file.txt claiming to be a particular user agent|fetch http://@host@/file.txt while claiming a particular user agent|download http://@host@/file.txt, spoofing the user agent string}
a: wget -U xos http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget -U
hint: -U overrides the user-agent string wget sends
---
q: {give up on http://@host@/file.txt if the server has not answered in 10 seconds|abandon a download of http://@host@/file.txt if the server stays silent for 10 seconds|cap the wait for http://@host@/file.txt at 10 seconds}
a: wget -T 10 http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget -T
hint: -T caps how long wget waits for the server before giving up
---
q: {write the download log for http://@host@/file.txt to a file instead of the screen|redirect the download log for http://@host@/file.txt to a file rather than the screen|log a download of http://@host@/file.txt to a file instead of printing it}
a: wget -o wget.log http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget -o
hint: -o redirects wget's log messages into a file
---
q: {download https://@host@/file.txt without checking the certificate -- and know why that is bad|fetch https://@host@/file.txt with certificate checking disabled -- and know why that is dangerous|skip certificate verification when downloading https://@host@/file.txt -- and understand the cost}
a: wget --no-check-certificate https://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget --no-check-certificate
hint: this turns off the only thing proving you are talking to who you think
---
q: {open a raw tcp connection to @host@ on port @port@|connect directly over tcp to @host@ on port @port@|use nc to open a raw connection to @host@:@port@}
a: nc @host@ @port@
why: live-net
ref: nc
teach: nc
hint: nc with just a host and port opens a raw tcp connection
---
q: {speak http by hand to @host@ -- send a HEAD request through nc|hand-craft a HEAD request to @host@ and send it through nc|talk http directly to @host@ by piping a HEAD request into nc}
a: printf 'HEAD / HTTP/1.0\r\n\r\n' | nc @host@ 80
why: live-net
ref: nc
teach: nc
hint: pipe a raw request into nc and read the raw response back
---
q: {connect to @host@ on port @port@ over telnet|open a telnet session to @host@ on port @port@|use telnet to connect to @host@ on port @port@}
a: telnet @host@ @port@
why: live-net
ref: telnet
teach: telnet
hint: telnet HOST PORT opens a plain tcp session to that port
---
q: {telnet into @host@ passing a chosen login name for @user@|telnet into @host@, supplying the login name @user@ up front|connect to @host@ with telnet and supply the login name @user@}
a: telnet -l @user@ @host@
why: live-net
ref: telnet
teach: telnet -l
hint: -l sends a login name as soon as the connection opens
---
q: {telnet into @host@, logging in automatically as the current user|connect to @host@ with telnet and let it send your username for you|telnet to @host@ with automatic login}
a: telnet -a @host@
why: live-net
ref: telnet
teach: telnet -a
hint: -a hands the server $USER as the login name, no prompt
---
q: {fetch a file over tftp from @host@|download a file using tftp from @host@|use tftp to fetch a file from @host@}
a: tftp -g -r file.txt @host@
why: live-net
ref: tftp
teach: tftp -g
teach: tftp -r
hint: -g gets a file, -r names which one
---
q: {upload @txt@ over tftp to @host@|send @txt@ up over tftp to @host@|use tftp to upload @txt@ to @host@}
a: tftp -p -l @txt@ @host@
why: live-net
ref: tftp
teach: tftp -p
teach: tftp -l
hint: -p puts a file, -l names the local file to send
---
q: {fetch over tftp from @host@ using a chosen block size|tftp a file from @host@ using a block size you choose|fetch from @host@ over tftp, setting the transfer block size}
a: tftp -b 1024 -g -r file.txt @host@
why: live-net
ref: tftp
teach: tftp -b
hint: -b sets the block size used for the transfer
---
q: {fetch a file from the ftp server @host@|download a file from the ftp server @host@|use ftpget to pull a file from @host@}
a: ftpget @host@ local.txt remote.txt
why: live-net
ref: ftpget
teach: ftpget
hint: ftpget takes the local name then the remote name, in that order
---
q: {fetch from @host@ via ftp as @user@, with a password|download from the ftp server @host@, authenticating as @user@|use ftpget with the username @user@ to fetch from @host@}
a: ftpget -u @user@ -p secret @host@ local.txt remote.txt
why: live-net
ref: ftpget
teach: ftpget -u
teach: ftpget -p
hint: -u sets the username, -p the password
---
q: {fetch from @host@ on the non-standard ftp port @port@|download from the ftp server @host@ listening on port @port@|use ftpget against @host@ on the non-default port @port@}
a: ftpget -P @port@ @host@ local.txt remote.txt
why: live-net
ref: ftpget
teach: ftpget -P
hint: -P picks the port instead of ftp's default 21
---
q: {continue an interrupted ftp download from @host@|resume an ftp download from @host@ that got interrupted|pick an interrupted ftp download from @host@ back up}
a: ftpget -c @host@ local.txt remote.txt
why: live-net
ref: ftpget
teach: ftpget -c
hint: -c continues a download from where it stopped
---
q: {upload @txt@ to the ftp server @host@|send @txt@ up to the ftp server @host@|use ftpput to upload @txt@ to @host@}
a: ftpput @host@ remote.txt @txt@
why: live-net
ref: ftpput
teach: ftpput
hint: ftpput takes the remote name then the local name, in that order
---
q: {upload @txt@ to @host@ via ftp as @user@, with a password|upload @txt@ to the ftp server @host@, authenticating as @user@|use ftpput with the username @user@ to upload @txt@ to @host@}
a: ftpput -u @user@ -p secret @host@ remote.txt @txt@
why: live-net
ref: ftpput
teach: ftpput -u
teach: ftpput -p
hint: -u sets the username, -p the password, same as ftpget
---
q: {upload @txt@ to @host@ on the non-standard ftp port @port@|upload @txt@ to the ftp server @host@ listening on port @port@|use ftpput against @host@ on the non-default port @port@ to send @txt@}
a: ftpput -P @port@ @host@ remote.txt @txt@
why: live-net
ref: ftpput
teach: ftpput -P
hint: -P picks the port instead of ftp's default 21
---
q: {ask for a dhcp lease on @iface@|request a dhcp lease on @iface@|use udhcpc to get a lease on @iface@}
a: udhcpc -i @iface@
why: live-net
ref: udhcpc
teach: udhcpc -i
hint: -i names which interface to request a lease on
---
q: {get a lease on @iface@ and exit rather than staying resident|grab a dhcp lease on @iface@ then quit, instead of staying resident|request a lease on @iface@ and exit immediately, no daemon}
a: udhcpc -i @iface@ -q
why: live-net
ref: udhcpc
teach: udhcpc -q
hint: -q exits once a lease is obtained instead of staying resident
---
q: {try three times on @iface@ before giving up|make three attempts at a lease on @iface@ before giving up|retry the dhcp request on @iface@ three times, then stop}
a: udhcpc -i @iface@ -t 3
why: live-net
ref: udhcpc
teach: udhcpc -t
hint: -t sets how many times to retry before giving up
---
q: {wait one second between lease attempts on @iface@|space dhcp retry attempts on @iface@ one second apart|pause one second between each retry on @iface@}
a: udhcpc -i @iface@ -T 1
why: live-net
ref: udhcpc
teach: udhcpc -T
hint: -T sets the pause between retry attempts, in seconds
---
q: {run the dhcp client on @iface@ in the foreground rather than backgrounding itself|keep udhcpc on @iface@ in the foreground instead of letting it daemonize|run the dhcp client on @iface@ in the foreground}
a: udhcpc -i @iface@ -f
why: live-net
ref: udhcpc
teach: udhcpc -f
hint: -f keeps udhcpc in the foreground instead of daemonizing
---
q: {exit rather than keep retrying @iface@ if no lease arrives|give up and exit if no lease ever arrives on @iface@|stop instead of retrying @iface@ forever when no lease shows up}
a: udhcpc -i @iface@ -n
why: live-net
ref: udhcpc
teach: udhcpc -n
hint: -n exits immediately if no lease shows up, rather than retrying forever
---
q: {run a chosen script on @iface@ when the lease is obtained|fire a script of your choosing on @iface@ once the lease arrives|have udhcpc run a script when @iface@ gets a lease}
a: udhcpc -i @iface@ -s /usr/share/udhcpc/default.script
why: live-net
ref: udhcpc
teach: udhcpc -s
hint: -s names a script to run once the lease is obtained
---
q: {release the current lease on @iface@ on exit|hand @iface@'s lease back to the server on exit|release the dhcp lease on @iface@ when udhcpc quits}
a: udhcpc -i @iface@ -R
why: live-net
ref: udhcpc
teach: udhcpc -R
hint: -R releases the lease back to the server when udhcpc exits
